-- | The signing-key publication interface of the Data Standard. A distributor
-- that delivers data by signature rather than by contract implements
-- `DistributorKey` on its own template, so a consumer can learn the public key
-- to check that distributor's signatures against without depending on the
-- distributor's package.
--
-- This package holds a view and one read choice, and no more than that.
-- Verification itself is library code in `canton-data-standard-codecs`,
-- executed inside the consumer's own choice. A package that defines a Daml
-- interface can never be smart-contract-upgraded, so any code placed here
-- would be unfixable; a bug in the library is repaired by a new version of it.
module DataStandard.DistributorKeyV1 where

-- | The on-ledger view every `DistributorKey` implementation exposes. It names
-- the party whose signatures the key validates, the key itself, and the three
-- identifiers a consumer needs to pick the matching off-ledger recipe: how the
-- payload is hashed, how the hash is signed, and which encoding the hash is
-- taken over.
--
-- The key is resident on a contract the distributor signs, which is what makes
-- it trustworthy: verification uses this key and not one the caller supplied.
-- Explicit disclosure of the contract is tamper-evident, because a contract id
-- commits to the contract's contents.
data DistributorKeyView = DistributorKeyView with
    distributor : Party
      -- ^ The party whose signatures this key validates, and whom the consumer
      -- chooses to trust as its source. Verification copies this field into its
      -- result, so a verified payload names an authenticated distributor and
      -- not one the payload itself claimed.
    publicKey : Text
      -- ^ The distributor's secp256k1 public key, hex-encoded DER (SPKI). One
      -- key authenticates every feed the distributor signs; rotation is
      -- replacement of the contract instance.
    signMethod : Text
      -- ^ The signature scheme the key is used with. `"secp256k1"` for every
      -- implementation the Data Standard currently defines.
    hashMethod : Text
      -- ^ The hash function the signature scheme is applied over. `"SHA-256"`
      -- for every implementation the Data Standard currently defines.
    payloadCodec : Text
      -- ^ The identifier of the encoding this key signs, for example
      -- `"v2-quote-hash"`. It selects the off-ledger encoder a distributor's
      -- signer must use and the library function a consumer verifies with; the
      -- library rejects a payload whose codec id does not match the one it
      -- implements. Products advertise their own ids here, so a codec that is
      -- not part of the Data Standard is named on the same field.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The signing-key publication interface. A distributor implements it on its
-- own template; consumers read the view through it and never import the
-- distributor's package.
--
-- Key rotation is instance replacement: archive the contract and create its
-- replacement holding the new key. A distributor's template adds its own
-- rotation choice for this. The interface defines none, because rotation policy
-- belongs to the distributor.
interface DistributorKey where
  viewtype DistributorKeyView

  -- | Read the view from a contract the caller can see. This is the read path
  -- for a party that is not a stakeholder of the contract: such a party obtains
  -- the contract through explicit disclosure, which grants visibility but not
  -- the authority to `fetch`, and so reads by exercising this choice, which the
  -- caller alone authorises. The choice is nonconsuming, and what it returns is
  -- the view, not the implementing template.
  nonconsuming choice DistributorKey_Fetch : DistributorKeyView
    with
      actor : Party
        -- ^ The party reading the key, and the sole controller of the choice.
    controller actor
    do pure (view this)
